window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-2c7e0d48f386c8e2"]={"key":"ny_main-2c7e0d48f386c8e2","group":"ny_main","uid":"2c7e0d48f386c8e2","rank":72,"title":"glassmere cross jurisdiction compliance assessment","occupation":"real_estate_brokers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are the commercial real-estate broker supporting Glassmere Media, and I need a decision-ready compliance assessment this week for Estelle Hartnett, with Lampwick Institute conducting the peer review. Glassmere is choosing between a New York studio and a Connecticut studio. The New York option is priced at $12,000 per month on a proposed five-year term with an intended 1 August 2027 move-in; the Connecticut option is $10,500 per month on a proposed three-year term with an intended 15 September 2027 move-in. The frozen jurisdiction snapshot is dated 30 June 2027: it records the municipal confirmation for the Connecticut site, but the confirmation required for the New York site is not present. The working record is also inconsistent. Ragnhild Sandoval's interview note dated 27 June describes commercial filming, occasional set construction, and an August move-in, while Estelle's later email describes post-production only and a September move-in. Aleksy Sheridan has already told Glassmere that the preferred option will be presented as ready to transact, so the wording must not turn an informal promise into evidence of eligibility or clearance. Prepare one PDF presentation comparing the two sites and their jurisdictions, reconciling the dated information, distinguishing documented facts from assumptions and unresolved eligibility, and giving one defensible preferred option with concrete matters for Lampwick Institute, Wheatcroft Chambers, and Willowmere Engineering to review. The audience includes people who know the transaction but not both jurisdictions, so make the decision and its limits clear without turning the presentation into a tutorial. The operating practice is changing from informal email-led clearance to a documented review gate; show what is supportable during that transition and what the durable end state should require. Stay within the New York and Connecticut material in this brief and the 30 June 2027 snapshot, do not invent municipal approvals, and do not commit Glassmere to a lease. Keep the presentation to no more than eight slides, use specific status language, and finish with the recommendation rather than a general process essay. Write the file into the top level of the working directory the solver starts in, using exactly the filename glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf` — A concise cross-jurisdiction compliance assessment presentation comparing the New York and Connecticut studio alternatives and stating one defensible preferred option. (Must be a readable PDF presentation of no more than eight slides.; Must be written for legal counsel and contracting stakeholders, including readers who lack background in both jurisdictions.; Must distinguish documented facts, assumptions, unresolved eligibility, and transaction readiness.; Must not invent municipal confirmation or commit Glassmere Media to a lease.; Must end with a clear recommendation, not an open-ended process discussion.; Must be saved at the top level of the starting working directory with exactly the specified filename.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf","bytes":20477,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/2be948a32154503c5ff691694b0287f3f5c6cf34e4f7ca8248d485d49fc053da.pdf","sha256":"2be948a32154503c5ff691694b0287f3f5c6cf34e4f7ca8248d485d49fc053da","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"CROSS-JURISDICTION COMPLIANCE ASSESSMENT\n\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media studio decision\nDecision-ready assessment for legal and contracting review\n\nPrepared for Estelle Hartnett | Peer review: Lampwick Institute\n\n\n\n       DECISION THIS WEEK                                                                                                  SCOPE BOUNDARY\n\n\n\n     Choose a defensible route for review                                                                                 AS-OF RECORD\n\n                                                                                                                          Frozen snapshot: 30 June 2027\n     The task is to identify a preferred studio path for continued diligence. It is not a lease award,\n     clearance finding, or authorization to transact.\n                                                                                                                          JURISDICTIONS\n\n                                                                                                                          New York and Connecticut only\n\n\n\n\n       ASSESSMENT CALL\n\n\n\n     Connecticut is preferred for gated review.\n     It is not yet ready to transact. New York remains unresolved because the required municipal confirmation is absent from the 30 June snapshot.\n\n\n\n\nSource boundary: supplied proposed terms, dated working record, and frozen snapshot; no municipal approval is inferred.                                   1/8\n\fDECISION COMPARISON\n\n\nComparison at the decision point\nCommercial terms are proposed terms. Municipal status is governed by the frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027.\n\n\n\n\n   COMPARISON ITEM                                   NEW YORK STUDIO                                               CONNECTICUT STUDIO\n\n\n  JURISDICTION                                       New York                                                      Connecticut\n\n\n  PROPOSED BASE RENT                                 $12,000 / month                                               $10,500 / month\n\n\n  ANNUALIZED BASE RENT                               $144,000 / year                                               $126,000 / year\n\n\n  CALCULATION BASIS                                  Monthly rent x 12                                             Monthly rent x 12\n\n\n  PROPOSED TERM                                      5 years                                                       3 years\n\n\n  PROPOSED MOVE-IN                                   1 August 2027                                                 15 September 2027\n\n\n  SNAPSHOT CONFIRMATION                              Required confirmation absent                                  Municipal confirmation recorded\n\n\n  INITIAL COMPLIANCE STATUS                          Unresolved; not ready to transact                             Supports gated review; not ready to transact\n\n\n\n\n   Cost delta: Connecticut is $1,500/month and $18,000/year lower.\n   That advantage and the shorter term do not settle permitted use, possession timing, or transaction readiness.\n\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                2/8\n\fEVIDENCE RECONCILIATION\n\n\nDated record: preserve the conflict\nA later statement orders the working record; it does not independently verify use, availability, or eligibility.\n\n\n\n\nKNOWN ORDER IN THE WORKING RECORD\n                                                               27 June interview                                                                later email (exact date not stated)\n\n\n\n\n      SOURCE STATEMENT                                                                                                           LATER CLIENT STATEMENT\n\n\n    27 JUNE 2027                                                                                                               LATER THAN 27 JUNE; EXACT DATE NOT STATED\n\n    Ragnhild Sandoval - Interview note                                                                                         Estelle Hartnett - Email\n    Use stated: Commercial filming with occasional set construction.                                                           Use stated: Post-production only.\n    Timing stated: August move-in.                                                                                             Timing stated: September move-in.\n\n\n\n\n      DOCUMENTED RECORD                     30 June 2027 frozen jurisdiction snapshot\n                                            Connecticut municipal confirmation recorded | Required New York confirmation absent\n\n\n\n\n   RELIANCE JUDGMENT               Retain both use and timing positions as contradictory. Recency is not proof of permitted use, property availability, or municipal eligibility.\n\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                                    3/8\n\fSTATUS FRAMEWORK\n\n\nStatus terms keep the decision bounded\nEach label below has a separate meaning. No label is a substitute for the documented review gate.\n\n\n\n\n                                         A supplied proposed term or a status actually recorded in the 30 June snapshot; it is not a blanket compliance conclusion.\n   DOCUMENTED FACT\n\n\n\n                                         Annualized base rent equals monthly rent x 12 and excludes escalations, taxes, fees, build-out, and other unprovided terms.\n   ASSUMPTION\n\n\n\n                                         A material item not settled by the record: permitted use, August versus September operating timing, and New York's required confirmation.\n   UNRESOLVED\n\n\n\n                                         A conclusion that must be supported by applicable review of evidence; an interview note, client email, or broker promise does not establish it.\n   ELIGIBLE\n\n\n\n                                         A separate transaction state reached only after documented, reviewable evidence and required review gates; it is not created by being preferred.\n   READY TO TRANSACT\n\n\n\n     NEW YORK                                                                                                                 CONNECTICUT\n\n   Required confirmation is absent as of the snapshot. This is an unresolved                                                Municipal confirmation is recorded. Permitted-use wording and possession still require\n   eligibility/compliance gap, not proof that the site is unlawful or unusable.                                             confirmation; the site is preferred for gated review, not ready to transact.\n\n\n\n\n                                                        Neither the interview note, later email, nor a commercial promise obtains, waives, or replaces the absent New York record.\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                                                                   4/8\n\fTRADE-OFFS\n\n\nTrade-offs are commercial and compliance-linked\nThe lower Connecticut rent and shorter term help the comparison, but do not independently produce a compliance or suitability conclusion.\n\n\n\n\n                                             WHAT THE RECORD SHOWS                                                                         DECISION CONSEQUENCE\n   COST AND TERM\n                                             Connecticut: $10,500/month, $126,000/year, 3 years. New York: $12,000/month,                  CT is lower by $1,500/month and $18,000/year, with a shorter proposed\n                                             $144,000/year, 5 years.                                                                       commitment; price and term remain only one decision factor.\n\n\n\n\n                                             WHAT THE RECORD SHOWS                                                                         DECISION CONSEQUENCE\n   USE FIT\n                                             Two unreconciled operations remain in the record: commercial filming with occasional          No site has a verified operational-use match in this record. Treat suitability\n                                             set construction, and post-production only.                                                   as unresolved until the proposed use is confirmed.\n\n\n\n\n                                             WHAT THE RECORD SHOWS                                                                         DECISION CONSEQUENCE\n   TIMING\n                                             Proposed terms identify 1 August 2027 for New York and 15 September 2027 for                  If August access is essential, the later CT date materially changes this\n                                             Connecticut; the working record separately conflicts on August versus September.              week's choice. An earlier NY date does not cure its missing confirmation.\n\n\n\n\n                                             WHAT THE RECORD SHOWS                                                                         DECISION CONSEQUENCE\n   EVIDENCE STATUS\n                                             The 30 June snapshot records Connecticut's confirmation and lacks the required New            CT has the stronger documented starting point. It still requires use and\n                                             York confirmation.                                                                            timing gates; NY cannot be represented as ready from the current record.\n\n\n\n\nDecision sensitivity: an unconfirmed operational need for August is an assumption to test, not a reason to re-label New York as cleared.\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                                                                          5/8\n\fPEER-REVIEW GATE\n\n\nReview gate: ownership and dependencies\nAll entries below are requested review matters. None is represented as complete.\n\n\n\n\n  OWNER                                              REVIEW NOW                                                                                           RESULT / DEPENDENCY\n\n\n   Lampwick Institute                                Peer-review both stated operations against the proposed sites; verify the CT-recorded /               Written view that preserves unresolved permitted use,\n   Peer review                                       NY-absent snapshot mapping; assess August versus September sensitivity.                               confirmation status, and timing before the route is\n                                                                                                                                                           described beyond gated review.\n\n\n\n\n   Wheatcroft Chambers                               Review evidence-supported status wording and the lease/condition implications of use,                 Defensible wording and legal conditions. This review\n   Legal review                                      timing, and any clearance representation.                                                             does not confirm property-side facts.\n\n\n\n\n   Willowmere Engineering                            Confirm site-side operational accommodation for filming/set construction and                          Factual use, 1 Aug / 15 Sep timing, and\n   and property-side contact                         post-production; confirm proposed possession and source records.                                      municipal-record evidence needed before any cleared\n                                                                                                                                                           or ready representation.\n\n\n\n\n     GATE RULE                        Do not switch either site to 'cleared' or 'ready to transact' until required evidence is documented, reviewable, and the designated reviews are complete.\n\n\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                                                                 6/8\n\fOPERATING TRANSITION\n\n\nTransition control: interim position and durable gate\nThis is practical current-transaction guidance, not a new policy manual or a permanent reliance on email-led clearance.\n\n\n\n\n      TEMPORARY OPERATING POSITION                                                                                               DURABLE END STATE\n\n\n\n\n    This transaction                                                                                                           Documented review gate\n    Keep a single evidence record that labels the frozen snapshot, interview note,                                             Before a site is represented as cleared or ready: retain reviewable\n    later email, and broker/client assertions. The only supportable interim wording                                            municipal/use evidence, confirm permitted-use wording and possession,\n    is 'preferred for documented review'. Informal emails do not become the                                                    record legal and property-side review, and capture an explicit approval to use\n    control or final clearance.                                                                                                the status label.\n\n\n\n\n      COMMUNICATION RISK\n\n\n\n    Aleksy Sheridan's prior 'ready to transact' representation must be qualified.\n    It creates an expectation, not evidence of eligibility or clearance. Correct the message to state that the preferred option is pending the documented review gate; no interview, email, or broker\n    promise authorizes a lease.\n\n\n                                                                          This assessment does not execute, authorize, or commit Glassmere Media to a lease.\n\n\nGlassmere Media | Frozen snapshot as-of 30 June 2027 | New York + Connecticut only                                                                                                                              7/8\n\fRECOMMENDATION\n\n\n\nConnecticut, conditional on the review gate\nSingle preferred route for Glassmere Media's decision this week\n\n\n\n\n    PREFERRED OPTION\n\n\n\n\n   Connecticut studio for continued review\n   Why this route: the 30 June snapshot records Connecticut's municipal confirmation. Proposed terms are $10,500/month, $126,000/year, 3 years, and 15 September 2027. New York is\n   $12,000/month, $144,000/year, 5 years; its required confirmation is absent.\n\n\n\n\n    CONDITIONS BEFORE CLEARANCE                                                                                                              NEW YORK STATUS\n\n\n\n  1. Resolve permitted-use fit                                                                                                             Keep New York unresolved\n      Lampwick peer review and property-side confirmation must resolve filming/set construction versus post-production-only use.\n                                                                                                                                           The required confirmation was absent as of 30 June. No\n                                                                                                                                           email, interview, or broker promise changes that record or\n  2. Confirm timing and legal conditions\n                                                                                                                                           makes the site ready.\n      Willowmere/property-side confirmation of 15 September possession and Wheatcroft review of conditions/status wording are\n      required.\n\n\n\n\n  Recommendation for gated review only: Connecticut is preferred, subject to closure of the documented review gate.\n  It is not yet ready to transact.                                                                          This assessment neither authorizes, executes, nor commits Glassmere Media to a lease.\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is present with the exact filename glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Working-directory file listing.","grader_hint":"Check the filename and extension exactly; do not award this for a renamed or differently placed file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PDF opens and renders as a coherent presentation without missing pages, unreadable text, or broken objects.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered PDF inspection.","grader_hint":"Open and render the PDF offline. Award only if the content can be reviewed normally."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than eight slides or PDF pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PDF page count.","grader_hint":"Count rendered pages; a document that exceeds the cap fails this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies Glassmere Media's leasing decision and names legal or contracting review as the audience or use.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening or framing content.","grader_hint":"Look for both the business decision and the intended review audience."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both the New York and Connecticut alternatives are evaluated, and each is tied to its jurisdiction rather than presented as an undifferentiated property choice.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison content.","grader_hint":"Verify that each site has identifiable treatment of its jurisdictional context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The 30 June 2027 as-of date is stated and visibly governs the status of the jurisdictional information.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"As-of statement or status legend.","grader_hint":"A generic date or undated reference is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison reports the stated monthly rents accurately: New York $12,000 ±$1 and Connecticut $10,500 ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial comparison.","grader_hint":"Check the displayed monthly figures against the brief with the stated tolerance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison preserves the different proposed terms and move-in dates: New York five years and 1 August 2027; Connecticut three years and 15 September 2027.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Site comparison.","grader_hint":"Check both duration and possession timing for both sites."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"If annualized base rent is calculated, the figures are correct at New York $144,000 ±$1 and Connecticut $126,000 ±$1, with the calculation basis clear.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Displayed calculation or comparison.","grader_hint":"Award when the annualized figures are correct and visibly derived from the monthly rents; do not require a particular cell or layout."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation separately captures the two conflicting permitted-use descriptions: commercial filming with occasional set construction versus post-production only.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Use-status comparison or chronology.","grader_hint":"Both descriptions must be retained; collapsing them into one generic use loses the conflict."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation separately captures the conflicting move-in expectations of August and September rather than reporting only one date as settled.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chronology or timing comparison.","grader_hint":"Look for both timing positions and their unresolved status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The dated record is reconciled in temporal order, including the 27 June interview note and the later Estelle email, without implying that recency alone proves the underlying permitted use or availability.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dated chronology and interpretation.","grader_hint":"Check that dates are ordered and that the document distinguishes a later statement from verified eligibility."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The treatment of the conflicting record is defensible: the presentation explains what can be relied on, what remains contradictory, and what must be confirmed before a compliance conclusion is treated as settled.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reviewer judgment from chronology and status language.","grader_hint":"A simple summary of both messages without a reliability or unresolved-status judgment does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation states that the Connecticut site's municipal confirmation is recorded in the frozen snapshot while the required New York confirmation is absent.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional status comparison.","grader_hint":"Verify the mapping of confirmation status to the correct site."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The absent New York confirmation is described as an unresolved eligibility or compliance gap, not as evidence that the site is unlawful or definitely unusable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"New York status wording.","grader_hint":"Look for a bounded statement of uncertainty rather than an unsupported legal conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation distinguishes municipal or use eligibility from readiness to transact, showing that documented confirmation is necessary but does not by itself settle every transaction condition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status framework or recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"Award only when the two concepts are explicitly separated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation does not state or imply that the missing New York confirmation has been obtained, waived, or replaced by an email, interview note, or commercial promise.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims audit.","grader_hint":"Check all references to New York clearance and confirm that none upgrades the missing record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Jurisdictional conclusions are confined to New York and Connecticut and to the 30 June 2027 snapshot, with no unsupported expansion to other laws, municipalities, or current-law thresholds.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope and legal-language review.","grader_hint":"Credit bounded scope; unsupported extra jurisdictions or current legal assertions fail this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies the origin or status of material statements, distinguishing the frozen snapshot, the dated interview note, the later email, and broker or client assertions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, footnotes, chronology, or evidence register.","grader_hint":"The document need not use a prescribed citation style, but provenance must be intelligible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Assumptions used to compare the alternatives are explicitly marked and are not presented as established facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions or limitations content.","grader_hint":"Look for clear qualifiers around inferred suitability, timing, or commercial implications."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies the material unknowns that could change the comparison, including unresolved use, municipal confirmation, and move-in timing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Open-issues or limitations content.","grader_hint":"All three categories must be visible; generic language such as 'some items remain open' is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The two sites are compared for suitability to the stated studio use, including the difference between commercial filming/set construction and post-production-only use.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Use-fit comparison.","grader_hint":"The comparison must address operational fit, not only price and dates."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation explains the practical consequence of the August versus September possession timing for Glassmere's decision this week.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit connection between timing and decision usability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The cost comparison acknowledges that the lower Connecticut rent and shorter term are not by themselves a complete compliance or suitability conclusion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial and compliance synthesis.","grader_hint":"Award when financial advantage is weighed alongside use, timing, and confirmation status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison makes the trade-offs between cost, term, timing, intended use, and documented confirmation understandable to a contracting stakeholder.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall comparison quality.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a stakeholder can see why the alternatives differ and what remains unresolved."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation states one clear preferred option rather than presenting two equal choices or several competing recommendations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation section or concluding slide.","grader_hint":"A conditional recommendation can still be singular; multiple unranked options do not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The preferred option is the Connecticut site, or the document provides an equally explicit, evidence-based explanation for why a different conclusion is more defensible despite the recorded confirmation gap.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"The default defensible compliance preference is Connecticut because its municipal confirmation is recorded; award an alternative only when the artifact directly addresses that evidence and justifies the departure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation's quantitative rationale agrees with the displayed rent, annualized cost, term, and possession figures, with no contradiction between comparison and conclusion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-check of financial and recommendation content.","grader_hint":"Recompute any displayed figures and compare them with the stated recommendation rationale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The preferred option is presented as conditional or subject to stated review gates rather than unconditionally 'ready to transact.'","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation wording.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear boundary between preferred for review and cleared for transaction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document handles Aleksy Sheridan's prior promise responsibly by acknowledging the communication risk without allowing it to determine compliance status.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk or recommendation treatment.","grader_hint":"The artifact should correct or qualify the promise, not ignore it and not repeat it as fact."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation gives concrete next matters for Lampwick Institute's peer review, including verification of permitted use, confirmation status, and timing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Peer-review request content.","grader_hint":"Next matters must be specific enough for a reviewer to act on, not merely 'review the deck.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The peer-review content distinguishes questions for Wheatcroft Chambers from matters requiring confirmation by Willowmere Engineering or the relevant property-side contact.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ownership or review matrix.","grader_hint":"Check that legal interpretation and property-side factual confirmation are not conflated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The next matters identify dependencies or timing significance for the decision this week without claiming that those matters have already been completed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Open-items and timing content.","grader_hint":"Award for actionable sequencing or dependency language that preserves the unresolved status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation clearly states that it does not itself execute, authorize, or commit Glassmere to a lease.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope, limitation, or recommendation language.","grader_hint":"The boundary must be explicit enough to prevent the deck being mistaken for authorization."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition-period treatment is described as a temporary operating position that preserves uncertainty rather than treating informal emails as the permanent control.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition-state content.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear interim status and an explanation of its limited force."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The durable end state is described as requiring documented, reviewable evidence before a site is represented as cleared or ready.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state control content.","grader_hint":"The end state must go beyond 'improve the process' and identify documented evidence and reviewability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The transition and end-state discussion is practical for the current transaction while remaining consistent with the future documented review gate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall normative guidance.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the artifact could guide this week's review without confusing temporary handling with final policy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A reader unfamiliar with both jurisdictions can understand the decision, evidence status, and limitations without needing the author to explain the deck orally.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document usability.","grader_hint":"Assess clarity for the less-backgrounded audience member, not merely the legal expert."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation remains an assessment and decision aid rather than becoming a jurisdictional tutorial or a long procedural manual.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document scope and density.","grader_hint":"Award when explanation is sufficient for the decision but does not overwhelm it with generic instruction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Terms such as confirmed, unresolved, eligible, preferred, and ready to transact are used with distinct and unambiguous meanings.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels and prose.","grader_hint":"Check whether status words are used consistently and not interchangeably."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Material facts, interpretations, assumptions, and open questions are visually or verbally distinguishable throughout the presentation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status labels, legends, or structured prose.","grader_hint":"A single disclaimer at the end is insufficient if the body repeatedly blurs categories."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Personal and sensitive information is minimized to what is necessary for accountability, with no unnecessary private identifiers or invented contact details.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document content review.","grader_hint":"Names supplied in the brief may be used; unrelated personal data should not appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The same site, jurisdiction, date, rent, term, possession timing, and confirmation status remain consistent wherever repeated in the presentation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document consistency check.","grader_hint":"Compare repeated facts across tables, narrative, and recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation ends with the single preferred option and its gating qualification, rather than ending on only a list of next steps or unresolved questions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final slide or final substantive paragraph.","grader_hint":"The final substantive message must contain the call and its boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Overall, the artifact is concise, professional, evidence-disciplined, and usable as a peer-review document without overstating what the record supports.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Holistic review of the rendered PDF.","grader_hint":"Use professional judgment on whether the deck could be sent to counsel or a contracting stakeholder with only minor editorial changes."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a broker-prepared compliance assessment for a small commercial leasing decision where jurisdictional eligibility and transaction readiness are not yet equivalent. The artifact must remain useful during a transition from informal email-led clearance to a documented review gate.","difficulty_sources":["Conflicting dated statements about permitted use and move-in timing require temporal reconciliation.","One jurisdiction has documented municipal confirmation while the other has a material documentary gap.","The artifact must distinguish eligibility, evidence status, and transaction readiness.","A prior informal promise creates pressure to overstate readiness.","The audience has uneven jurisdictional background and the operating practice is mid-transition.","The solver must compare, model the financial facts, synthesize the evidence, and make a bounded professional call without external sources."],"authoring_plan":{"source_strategy":"Create no reference files. Treat the facts embedded in the request as the complete working record: New York is $12,000 per month for five years with an intended 1 August 2027 move-in and missing municipal confirmation; Connecticut is $10,500 per month for three years with an intended 15 September 2027 move-in and recorded municipal confirmation. Treat the 27 June interview note, the later Estelle email, and the 30 June 2027 frozen snapshot as distinct dated evidence categories.","gold_content":{"framing":"The gold presentation should be a concise broker compliance assessment for Glassmere Media, clearly scoped to New York and Connecticut and dated to the frozen snapshot.","comparison_logic":["Preserve both alternatives and compare rent, annualized base rent, term, possession timing, intended use, evidence status, and transaction-readiness implications.","Annualized base rent should be New York $144,000 and Connecticut $126,000, with the basis visible if calculated.","Do not treat the shorter Connecticut term or lower rent as sufficient by itself; weigh commercial fit, timing, use, and documentary support."],"temporal_reconciliation":["Show the 27 June interview note before the later Estelle email.","Retain the conflict between commercial filming with occasional set construction and post-production only.","Retain the August versus September move-in conflict.","Explain that chronology helps identify the current record but does not itself prove permitted use, availability, or municipal eligibility."],"jurisdictional_reasoning":["Connect the recorded confirmation to Connecticut and the absent required confirmation to New York.","Describe New York as unresolved rather than unlawful, rejected, or cleared by implication.","Keep all legal or compliance statements bounded to the supplied snapshot and avoid invented statutory thresholds, approvals, or waivers."],"intended_verdict":"The preferred compliance route should ordinarily be Connecticut because its municipal confirmation is recorded and its lower base rent and shorter term are supported facts, but it must be presented as conditional and not ready to transact unconditionally. The gold may explain that the September possession date must be reconciled with Glassmere's needs and that permitted-use wording still requires review. New York should not be represented as ready while the required municipal confirmation is absent.","promise_handling":"Acknowledge Aleksy Sheridan's prior representation as a communication and expectation risk. The gold must explicitly prevent that promise from becoming evidence of clearance or authorization.","peer_review":["Identify concrete review questions for Lampwick Institute.","Separate legal interpretation or wording review for Wheatcroft Chambers from factual property-side confirmation involving Willowmere Engineering.","State dependencies and timing significance without claiming that review or confirmation has already occurred."],"transition_end_state":["Describe a bounded interim treatment for the current transaction that preserves unresolved status and prevents informal email assertions from functioning as final clearance.","Describe a durable end state in which documented, reviewable evidence and an explicit review gate support any representation that a site is cleared or ready.","Keep the transition discussion practical and normative, not a generic tutorial or click-by-click process."],"presentation_quality":["Use distinct labels for fact, assumption, unresolved item, preferred option, and transaction readiness.","Keep the artifact within the requested eight-slide maximum.","End on one preferred option with its gating qualification, not merely on open questions or next steps."]},"rubric_derivation":"Score factual fidelity, correct calculations, temporal reconciliation, bounded jurisdictional reasoning, evidence discipline, conditional recommendation, treatment of the informal promise, concrete peer-review matters, and the transition/end-state distinction. All rubric items are credit items; omissions simply do not earn their associated credit."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"compliance_assessment_presentation","filename":"glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A concise cross-jurisdiction compliance assessment presentation comparing the New York and Connecticut studio alternatives and stating one defensible preferred option.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be a readable PDF presentation of no more than eight slides.","Must be written for legal counsel and contracting stakeholders, including readers who lack background in both jurisdictions.","Must distinguish documented facts, assumptions, unresolved eligibility, and transaction readiness.","Must not invent municipal confirmation or commit Glassmere Media to a lease.","Must end with a clear recommendation, not an open-ended process discussion.","Must be saved at the top level of the starting working directory with exactly the specified filename."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"compliance_assessment","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"real_estate_brokers","sector":"real_estate_rental_leasing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use one domain-expert review and one independent presentation-quality review. The domain reviewer should verify the jurisdictional-status reasoning, temporal reconciliation, transaction-readiness boundaries, and transition/end-state controls. The independent reviewer should verify that the recommendation, limitations, and peer-review asks are understandable from the PDF alone.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No external source files are supplied; the factual record is contained in the request and the professional standard supplies the expected compliance-discipline judgment.","The assessment should not assert municipal or current-law facts beyond the frozen 30 June 2027 record."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:40:13+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:40:13+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:40:13+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:40:13+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/2c7e0d48f386c8e2","declared_deliverables":["glassmere_cross_jurisdiction_compliance_assessment.pdf"]};
